#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179503 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ed80030-2c39-5b66-a3c5-fb1a8428a780" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Function Over an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "5ebfa0d1-bf16-56d0-a58f-2b16720b7aad" :question/math-academy-id "e-9439" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral $\\displaystyle\\int_\\limits{C}12xy\\:\\textrm{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is the quarter-ellipse $4x^2+y^2=4$ traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(-1,0)$, as shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3690/Source/Images/68d8dbac3c41c99668f956642b8e26fd.png)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\nx^2 + \\dfrac{y^2}{2^2} = 1\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $2$, respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\le t \\le \\pi\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-\\sin ti + 2\\cos tj \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-\\sin t)^{2} + (2\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\sin^{2} t + 4\\cos^{2} t} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3\\cos^{2} t + (\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + 3\\cos^{2} t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 12xy\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= 12 \\cdot \\cos t \\cdot 2\\sin t = 24\\sin t\\cos t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)12xyds &= ∫_{π/2}^{π}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{π/2}^{π}24\\sin t\\cos t \\cdot \\sqrt{1 + 3\\cos^{2} t} \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= 24∫_{π/2}^{π}\\sin t\\cos t\\sqrt{1 + 3\\cos^{2} t}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution\n\n$$\nu=1+3\\cos^2 {t}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\textrm d u=-6\\sin t\\cos t\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n24∫_{π/2}^{π}\\sin t\\cos t\\sqrt{1 + 3\\cos^{2} t}dt &= -4∫_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{u}du \\\\\n&=-4 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2} \\mid _{1}^{4} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{8}{3}(4^{3/2} - 1^{3/2}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{8}{3}(8 - 1) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{56}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179508 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "440cb768-be67-5dd3-98c8-6a58f060245c" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "1990e97c-7244-5b6d-a5d5-69cd4b91ad21" :question/math-academy-id "e-9460" :question/problem "What expression is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_\\limits{C} \\dfrac{x}{y} \\: \\textrm{d}s$, where $C$ is the ellipse $x^2 + 16y^2 = 16$, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{4^2} + \\dfrac{y^2}{1^2} = 1\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $4$ and $1$, respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle 4\\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t} \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(4\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-4\\sin ti + \\cos tj \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-4\\sin t)^{2} + (\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{15\\sin^{2} t + (\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{15\\sin^{2} t + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= \\frac{4\\cos t}{\\sin t} = 4\\cot t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)\\frac{x}{y}ds &= ∫_{0}^{2π}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}4\\cot t\\sqrt{15\\sin^{2} t + 1}dt \\\\\n&= 4∫_{0}^{2π}\\cot t\\sqrt{15\\sin^{2} t + 1}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179518 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08dc697e-fd67-5ae0-889b-ea81c69e2277" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Part-Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "239120b8-0553-5cff-aee1-f50253af11ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-9488" :question/problem "Find, as a definite integral, an expression for the line integral of the function $\\displaystyle\\int_\\limits{C} 2xy \\:\\textrm{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is the right half of the ellipse $9x^2 + 4y^2 = 36$, shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3690/Source/Images/372d3096b2e6ec488c80ec89998d7543.png)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{2^2} + \\dfrac{y^2}{3^2} = 1\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $3$, respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 3\\sin{t} \\right\\rangle, \\qquad -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\le t \\le \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(3\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-2\\sin ti + 3\\cos tj \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-2\\sin t)^{2} + (3\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4\\sin^{2} t + 9\\cos^{2} t} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{5\\cos^{2} t + 4(\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{5\\cos^{2} t + 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2xy\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= 2(2\\cos t)(3\\sin t) \\\\\n&= 12\\sin t\\cos t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)2xyds &= ∫_{- π/2}^{π/2}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{- π/2}^{π/2}12\\sin t\\cos t\\sqrt{5\\cos^{2} t + 4}dt \\\\\n&= 12∫_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\sin t\\cos t\\sqrt{5\\cos^{2} t + 4}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08dc697e-fd67-5ae0-889b-ea81c69e2277" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Part-Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3d98f01-3e7e-59b3-aaf5-c5ce720397cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{π/2}^{3π/2}\\tan t\\sqrt{15\\sin^{2} t + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177359} {:answer/id #uuid "0e078cf4-8f30-5cc5-9a06-a4f2c410b003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{π/2}^{3π/2}4\\tan t\\sqrt{5\\sin^{2} t + 3}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177360} {:answer/id #uuid "40052331-791c-5eb5-a754-97b28701b244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{- π/2}^{π/2}4\\cot t\\sqrt{\\sin^{2} t + 15}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177361} {:answer/id #uuid "4c32c48e-4817-5120-85bb-e4ce2beec376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\tan t\\sqrt{15\\sin^{2} t + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177362} {:answer/id #uuid "7293c964-eac2-5d87-976f-b0e6b8b8118f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{π/2}^{3π/2}\\cot t\\sqrt{5\\sin^{2} t + 3}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3d98f01-3e7e-59b3-aaf5-c5ce720397cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{π/2}^{3π/2}\\tan t\\sqrt{15\\sin^{2} t + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177359} :answer-field/id #uuid "76c96b53-25f3-59c6-822a-86b40b443839" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177358}] :question/id #uuid "939eabb6-2bce-5cdb-8479-d0b9ae63294a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122269" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3690/Source/Images/q-122269.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral $∫_(C)\\frac{4y}{x}ds$, where the curve $C$ is the left half of the ellipse $x^{2} + 16y^{2} = 16$, shown above?"}]}
